WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.368
Four years ago,

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:01.935
my husband

00:00:01.935 --> 00:00:03.003
Mike and I

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:05.171
were sitting in a hospital room

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:06.573
when our child, Abby,

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:09.009
was diagnosed with leukemia.

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:10.977
I think anybody who's been

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:13.079
in a life threatening

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:15.315
diagnosis, you know, will

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:19.219
remember those moments

00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:20.487
like go in.

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:21.755
She's going to be able to have our chemo,

00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:22.288
but she's not going

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:24.090
to have Erwinaze today.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.493
How come it's on shortage?

00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:27.927
Obvious, clever.

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:31.431
Nothing gets past her and she just said,

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:33.833
What does this mean?

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:35.835
Does this mean I die?

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:37.470
She asks you that question.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:37.937
Yeah.

00:00:37.937 --> 00:00:39.472
It's hard enough that my nine year

00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:41.508
old had to contemplate her mortality

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:43.076
when she was diagnosed with cancer

00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:44.611
and also then wondered

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:46.146
if she was going to survive

00:00:46.146 --> 00:00:49.649
because not enough drug was made

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:51.217
What she did next

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:53.420
makes her a true champion for change.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.889
She put together a consortium

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:57.090
of friends and family

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.192
and they essentially created a phone bank

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:01.428
calling more than 220 children's

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:03.063
hospitals around the country

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:05.098
and asking all of them did

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:06.699
they have the medication

00:01:06.699 --> 00:01:08.334
that could save Abby?

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:11.071
Finally, someone said yes,

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:12.906
it was a relief

00:01:12.906 --> 00:01:16.509
and a release of all the stress.

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:20.113
And then I felt tremendous guilt,

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:22.649
and I was haunted by the

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:26.086
knowledge that somewhere in the country

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:28.955
some other mom and child

00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:31.458
was going to be going through

00:01:31.458 --> 00:01:34.260
the same hopeless conversation.

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:37.664
So we launched Angels for Change,

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:38.465
and almost immediately

00:01:38.465 --> 00:01:40.467
people began to call.

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:42.235
So right now, we're in a ten year

00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:43.736
high for drug shortages.

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:45.505
There's more than 300

00:01:45.505 --> 00:01:47.540
essential medicine shortages.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:49.742
There's four key reasons why

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:51.044
there's shortage.

00:01:51.044 --> 00:01:51.678
The lower

00:01:51.678 --> 00:01:53.012
the price medicine,

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:55.181
the more likely it is to be in shortage.

00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:58.985
The more complicated the medicine is.

00:01:58.985 --> 00:02:01.287
There's a history of acute event

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:04.958
that all of it is made by one supplier

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:07.026
or one area of the world.

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:09.662
It's more likely to be in shortage.

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:10.263
I think we've gotten

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:11.331
so used to thinking

00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:12.999
about things like Amazon.

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:14.100
They can anticipate

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.435
when you're running out of toilet paper,

00:02:15.435 --> 00:02:17.070
when you're running out of dog food,

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.838
you think the same thing would happen

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:20.940
with lifesaving medications.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.041
But that's not the case.

00:02:23.243 --> 00:02:25.078
The entire supply chain

00:02:25.078 --> 00:02:25.979
has to be engaged.

00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:26.813
So I thought,

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:29.282
what would it look like to partner

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:33.153
with the manufacturer and use prediction?

00:02:33.353 --> 00:02:33.987
How do you get them?

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:34.554
She calls it

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:36.122
Project Protect,

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:37.023
anticipating

00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:38.658
which drugs could go into shortage

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:39.492
and producing them

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:40.527
with other companies

00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:42.595
before it's too late.

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:43.796
Their first effort,

00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:44.964
the $100,000

00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:46.266
grant to the for profit

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:47.767
stack pharmaceutical

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:48.701
to manufacture

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:50.370
two specific drugs,

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.739
potassium chloride and sodium chloride,

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:55.241
which newborn's non-ICU patients pick.

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:57.877
New patients need to survive

00:02:57.877 --> 00:02:59.312
It's like salt and pepper.

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:00.680
You never think you're going to run out

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.348
of those very simple things.

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:04.017
Well, don't take our supply chain

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:04.651
for granted.

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:06.286
Jared Milton oversees

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:07.020
all pharmacy

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.087
and clinical services

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:10.290
at Children's Hospital, Colorado.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.792
What Project Protect didn't exist?

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:14.127
What would have happened

00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:15.128
in that situation?

00:03:15.128 --> 00:03:16.429
I shudder to think about what

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:17.997
we would have had to do.

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:18.932
Potassium chloride,

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:20.366
one of the first drugs.

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:20.867
So you're able

00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:23.069
to anticipate this shortage

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:24.938
basically at risk.

00:03:24.938 --> 00:03:27.040
Create the medications

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.441
And they did go into shortage.

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:29.475
They did go into shortage.

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:32.579
And more than 700,000 treatments

00:03:32.612 --> 00:03:33.713
were accessed.

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:35.882
And what we know. 700,000.

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:37.350
Yeah. That's incredible.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:38.851
It is incredible.

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:40.053
And you see this now

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:42.021
or you see this coming off the line,

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:43.723
so to speak. What's that like for you?

00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:45.692
What I see here is each

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:47.460
one of those is being filled up.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.462
Seven to nine Nicky babies that

00:03:49.462 --> 00:03:50.563
are going to get fed today.

00:03:52.298 --> 00:03:54.867
How is Abby doing?

00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:57.036
She is officially a survivor.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:58.271
She's doing great.

00:03:58.271 --> 00:04:01.140
She's a very normal, typical,

00:04:01.541 --> 00:04:03.176
bright, clever, fierce.

00:04:03.176 --> 00:04:04.777
13 year old.

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:06.813
John, we report a lot on drug shortages

00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:07.480
and if you caught that.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.482
But 300 drugs in shortage

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:10.817
at the end of 2022.

00:04:10.817 --> 00:04:12.518
The average length of a shortage

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:13.953
is a year and a half.

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:14.754
I mean, it's

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:15.154
hard to believe

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:15.455
we spent

00:04:15.455 --> 00:04:16.990
$4 trillion on health care

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:17.857
in this country.

00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:18.791
And that's what we often

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:19.392
have to deal with.

00:04:19.392 --> 00:04:21.394
And it's a challenging problem to fix.

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:23.963
But to get a determined mom like that,

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:26.099
and she makes a difference in the system.

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:26.666
That's what we need.

00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:27.133
It's amazing,

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:29.435
wonderful that her daughter is doing okay

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:31.170
and now she wants to spread that

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:32.338
and keep it going for others

00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:33.573
and making such a difference.

00:04:33.573 --> 00:04:35.708
Sanjay, thank you. Great to see you.

00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:37.477
So The Champions for Change one

00:04:37.477 --> 00:04:40.179
hour special is Saturday night at 8 p.m.

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:40.680
Eastern.

